WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.080
On the 7th&nbsp;of May, France will hold the most important presidential election in the history of the Fifth Republic.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.660
After decades of elections fought between nearly identical center-left and center-right candidates,

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.800
the French have a choice between two political outsiders:

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.240
the centrist Emmanuel Macron and Marine Le Pen, the leader of the National Front.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.040
Macron, who never held elected office before, claims to be neither on the political left nor right.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.220
He promises deep economic reforms, liberalization,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.120
supports the European Union,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.740
and he's the only one of the four leading candidates to openly criticize Vladimir Putin and the Kremlin.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.380
A Le Pen presidency would be very different.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.740
At home, it would mean a return to heavy-handed industrial policy and protectionism.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.640
Internationally, it would mean cozying up to the Kremlin,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.480
which has supported Le Pen’s campaign both verbally and financially.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.540
It could mean France’s exit from the EU and from NATO

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.900
– essentially the end of the postwar political order in Europe.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.640
The choice facing French voters could not be starker.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.900
What do you think about the French election?

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.300
Let us know in your comments,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.780
and let us also know what other topics you'd like AEI scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

